  "hints": [
    "inc and dec change one count by exactly 1. A key never jumps over a count, which is the property to exploit.",
    "Keep a doubly linked list of buckets ordered by count, each holding the set of keys with that count. A key moves to the neighbouring bucket on inc or dec, creating or deleting buckets at the edges.",
    "The max key comes from the last bucket, the min key from the first. When dec takes a key's count to 0, remove the key entirely; an empty bucket is unlinked."
  ],
  "complexity": {"time":"O(1) for inc, dec, getMaxKey and getMinKey","space":"O(n) for n distinct keys"}
}
